# GTO Poker Trainer

A Texas Hold'em simulator and trainer built on a validated math engine: an
exact hand evaluator, an adaptive equity calculator, opponents that play off
pot odds, and a CFR+ river solver that reports its own exploitability.

## Run locally

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

```bash
npm test
```

The test suite is the point of this project — every claim below is checked by
it. Run it before trusting any number the UI shows you.

## The engine

Everything in `src/engine/` is pure computation with no DOM dependency, so it
runs identically on the main thread, in a Web Worker, or under Node.

| Module | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| [`evaluator.js`](src/engine/evaluator.js) | 5–7 card hand evaluation, ~59M hands/sec |
| [`equity.js`](src/engine/equity.js) | Exact enumeration when tractable, seeded Monte Carlo otherwise |
| [`range.js`](src/engine/range.js) | Range notation (`TT+`, `A5s+`, `22-55`, weights) → concrete combos |
| [`handStrength.js`](src/engine/handStrength.js) | 169 starting hands ranked by measured equity (generated) |
| [`opponent.js`](src/engine/opponent.js) | Bot decisions from equity vs pot odds |
| [`table.js`](src/engine/table.js) | No-limit betting: blinds, min raises, side pots, showdown |
| [`solver.js`](src/engine/solver.js) | CFR+ solver for flop, turn, and river, range vs range |
| [`mccfr.js`](src/engine/mccfr.js) | External-sampling MCCFR, game-agnostic |
| [`mccfrHoldem.js`](src/engine/mccfrHoldem.js) | Multiway preflop push/fold over the betting engine |

Cards are integers (`rank * 4 + suit`) on every hot path; strings are only for
display.

### Validation

The engine is checked against external ground truth, not against itself:

- **Evaluator** — all 2,598,960 five-card hands produce the textbook category
  frequencies (40 straight flushes, 624 quads, … 1,302,540 high cards), and
  30k random seven-card hands match an independently written brute-force
  reference on both classification and pairwise ordering.
- **Equity** — matches published all-in equities: AA vs KK 81.95%, AKs vs QQ
  46.21%, AKo vs 22 46.96%, AKo vs AQo 74.39% (suit-averaged), AA vs a random
  hand 85.09%. Monte Carlo lands within its own reported error bars of the
  exact answer.
- **Betting** — 3000 random hands and 20,659 actions with chips verified
  after *every single action*; worst drift 0.000000. Side pots, minimum
  raises, all-in-for-less not reopening betting, and the big blind's option
  are each checked directly.
- **Solver** — terminal values match a brute-force O(n·m) reference to 6e-14
  including exact card removal; exploitability falls from 2.3% to 0.03% of pot
  over 10 → 800 iterations; and the polarized-vs-bluffcatcher toy game
  reproduces its known analytic equilibrium (with a pot-sized bet the
  bluffcatcher calls 50.0% and the polarized player bluffs 50.0% of its air,
  matching P/(P+B)).
- **MCCFR** — checked against Kuhn poker, whose equilibrium is closed-form. It
  recovers the whole equilibrium family (P1 never bets the queen and bets the
  king exactly 3× as often as the jack; P2 mixes 1/3 with both), and the exact
  value of the solved profile is −0.05605 against the analytic −1/18.
  Exploitability, computed by enumerating all 64 pure strategies per player, is
  0.0042 chips/hand.
- **Multiway** — heads-up shoving matches published Nash charts (71.1% vs 71 at
  5bb, 59.6% vs 59 at 10bb). The sharper check needs no chart: the hands the
  big blind *mixes* between calling and folding must sit at the pot odds, and
  their mean equity against the solved shoving range is 44.0% against a
  theoretical 45.0%.
- **Chance nodes** — with betting disabled a subgame is pure showdown equity,
  so the solved value must equal `(pot/2)(2·equity−1)`. It matches the equity
  engine to 1e-6 on both the turn and a fully enumerated flop, which checks the
  runout handling against an independently validated component.

### Exploitability, not vibes

The solver reports how far from equilibrium a solve actually is, so its output
can be judged rather than taken on faith. Measured in-browser against the app's
own ranges (~180 vs ~250 combos), solving in a worker so the table stays
interactive:

| Street | Runouts | Time | Exploitable |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| River | 1 (exact) | 92ms | 0.60% of pot |
| Turn | 48 (exact) | 1.4s | 0.78% of pot |
| Flop | 24 (sampled) | 4.4s | 0.87% of pot |

### Multiway, and where it stops

Preflop is the one street that is reliably multiway, and the heads-up vector
solver cannot model it at all. MCCFR handles 2–6 players by running over the
same betting engine the table uses, so side pots and legality are not
reimplemented.

It is a **push/fold** model: shove or fold preflop, board checked down. That is
the right description of short-stack play and the wrong one of deep play, so it
is gated to 25bb and refuses to answer above that rather than giving confident
bad advice. Six-max at 12bb solves in ~8s to 0% undertrained, and is cached by
table shape so later hands return in milliseconds.

The sized-raise variant does **not** converge at six-max: at 200k iterations the
information set count is still growing and the median set has been visited
three times. `trainingQuality()` reports this, because under-training is
otherwise invisible — at 20k iterations a six-max "opening range" ranked Q7s
above JTs, which is noise wearing the shape of strategy.

One caveat worth stating plainly: for a sampled flop that percentage is
exploitability *within the runouts actually explored*. Against the full game it
is higher, and shrinking the sample makes the number look better while making
the answer worse. The UI says so on every flop solve.

## What is real and what is not

This codebase previously advertised capabilities it did not have, so to be
explicit:

- **Real:** hand evaluation, equity, ranges, opponent decisions, no-limit
  betting, and the river solver. All covered by `npm test`.
- **Heuristic:** the Rust service in `solver/` is a hand-strength estimate. It
  reports itself as such. It is *not* a CFR solver, despite once claiming to be
  "MCCFR (External Sampling)".
- **Abstracted:** flop solving explores a sample of runouts rather than all
  49×48 of them, which would need hundreds of megabytes. Results carry `exact:
  false` and the UI labels them.
- **Not implemented:** deep-stacked preflop solving, and multiway *postflop*.
  Preflop multiway is push/fold only; postflop remains heads-up, two ranges.

## Regenerating derived data

`src/engine/handStrength.js` is generated, not hand-written:

```bash
npm run gen:strength
```

## Layout

```
src/engine/      math and rules: evaluator, equity, ranges, opponents,
                 betting engine, solver (+ workers)
src/components/  SVG playing cards
src/utils/       thin compatibility shims over the engine
server/          Express + SQLite session and analytics store
solver/          Rust advice service (heuristic)
scripts/         data generators
```
